    "question_text": "Explain how React's virtual DOM improves rendering performance, and mention one case where it does not help.",
    "question_type": "essay",
    "complexity": "medium",
    "options": [],
    "correct_answer": "React keeps a lightweight in-memory tree of the UI. On state changes it builds a new tree, diffs it against the previous one (reconciliation) and applies only the minimal set of real DOM updates, batching them. It does not help when a component re-renders expensively on every change with large lists without keys or memoization, or when the cost is in computation or layout rather than in DOM writes.",
    "image_key": null,
    "created_by": {
      "$oid": "6b06f47cc37c2b5d330a522f"
    },
    "created_at": {
      "$date": "2026-10-18T16:59:27Z"
    },
    "updated_at": {
      "$date": "2026-10-18T16:59:27Z"
    }
  },
