    "question_text": "Explain what a database index is and the trade-offs of adding many indexes to a write-heavy table.",
    "question_type": "essay",
    "complexity": "high",
    "options": [],
    "correct_answer": "An index is an auxiliary data structure (commonly a B-tree) that lets the database find rows without scanning the whole table, speeding up lookups, joins and sorts. The trade-off is that every INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE must also maintain each index, which slows writes, and indexes consume extra storage. On write-heavy tables you should index only columns that are actually queried.",
    "image_key": null,
    "created_by": {
      "$oid": "6b06f47cc37c2b5d330a522f"
    },
    "created_at": {
      "$date": "2026-10-19T16:58:27Z"
    },
    "updated_at": {
      "$date": "2026-10-19T16:58:27Z"
    }
  },
